 the selected catalog that satisfies the voltage-drop model at the design current. It is intentionally not presented as a universal code-compliant cable size. Thermal ampacity depends on insulation rating, installation method, ambient temperature, grouping, terminals, local code rules and protection. Those checks cannot be inferred safely from voltage drop alone.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"article\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"font-black text-slate-950\",\"children\":\"AWG and kcmil\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-2 text-sm leading-6\",\"children\":\"US-style conductor sizes are converted through the standard AWG geometry and circular-mil relationship. The tool reports the actual cross-sectional area used in the resistance calculation.\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"article\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"font-black text-slate-950\",\"children\":\"Metric mm²\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-2 text-sm leading-6\",\"children\":\"Metric conductor recommendations use common standard cross-sections from 0.5 mm² through large feeder sizes. Parallel runs can be entered when a single conductor is impractical.\"}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"text-2xl font-black text-slate-950\",\"children\":\"Why a larger cable can save energy\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-3 leading-7\",\"children\":\"Larger conductors have lower resistance. That reduces I²R losses and can lower annual energy waste, especially on long runs with high utilization. The calculator therefore shows annual cable-loss energy and cost alongside the voltage-drop result so a designer can compare first-cost and operating-loss tradeoffs.\"}]]}]]}]\n14:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-1